assertion  XSH.fmod.001
anchor     functions/fmod.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/fmod.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      1988
says       fmod(5.5, 2), fmod(-5.5, 2), fmod(5.5, -2), fmod(-5.5, -2), fmod(1.5,
           4), fmod(-1.5, 4), fmod(6, 3), fmod(7, 2.5), fmod(0.75, 0.25) and
           fmod(100, 7) give 1.5, -1.5, 1.5, -1.5, 1.5, -1.5, 0, 2, 0 and 2.
test       t/001-values.c

assertion  XSH.fmod.002
anchor     functions/fmod.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/fmod.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      1988
says       With each rounding direction that can be selected (at least two are
           needed), fmod gives the same results as in the first assertion.
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Floating-Point option; the
           test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it (Annex F of the
           C standard).
test       t/002-rounding-mode.c

assertion  XSH.fmod.003
anchor     functions/fmod.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/fmod.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
says       fmod(NaN, 2), fmod(2, NaN), fmod(NaN, NaN) and fmod(-NaN, 2) return a
           NaN.
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Floating-Point option; the
           test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it (Annex F of the
           C standard).
test       t/003-nan.c

assertion  XSH.fmod.004
anchor     functions/fmod.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/fmod.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
says       fmod(NaN, 0), fmod(Inf, NaN), fmod(NaN, Inf) and fmod(0, NaN) return a
           NaN and are not domain errors: errno (set to EDOM beforehand) is left
           alone and the invalid exception is not raised.
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Floating-Point option; the
           test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it (Annex F of the
           C standard). A quiet NaN is used, which operations do not signal.
test       t/004-nan-first.c

assertion  XSH.fmod.005
anchor     functions/fmod.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/fmod.html#RETURN VALUE
anchor     functions/fmod.html#ERRORS
class      required
since      1988
says       For x equal to 5.5, -5.5 and 0, fmod(x, 0) returns a NaN and is a
           domain error, reported through errno (EDOM) and the invalid exception
           as math_errhandling selects.
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Floating-Point option; the
           test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it (Annex F of the
           C standard).
test       t/005-y-zero.c

assertion  XSH.fmod.006
anchor     functions/fmod.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/fmod.html#RETURN VALUE
anchor     functions/fmod.html#ERRORS
class      required
says       fmod(Inf, 2), fmod(-Inf, 2) and fmod(Inf, Inf) return a NaN and are
           domain errors, reported as math_errhandling selects.
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Floating-Point option; the
           test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it (Annex F of the
           C standard).
test       t/006-x-infinite.c

assertion  XSH.fmod.007
anchor     functions/fmod.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/fmod.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
says       fmod(+0, 2), fmod(-0, 2), fmod(+0, -2) and fmod(-0, -2) return +0, -0,
           +0 and -0.
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Floating-Point option; the
           test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it (Annex F of the
           C standard).
test       t/007-x-zero.c

assertion  XSH.fmod.008
anchor     functions/fmod.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/fmod.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
says       fmod(5.5, Inf), fmod(-5.5, Inf), fmod(5.5, -Inf) and fmod(-5.5, -Inf)
           return 5.5, -5.5, 5.5 and -5.5.
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Floating-Point option; the
           test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it (Annex F of the
           C standard).
test       t/008-y-infinite.c

assertion  XSH.fmod.009
anchor     functions/fmod.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/fmod.html#RETURN VALUE
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap04.html#4.23
class      required
since      1988
says       With errno holding EDOM before the call, fmod(5.5, 2) leaves it
           unchanged and, when exception flags are in use, raises none of the
           invalid, divide-by-zero and overflow exceptions.
test       t/009-no-error.c
